    "authorAssociation": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [X] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.pick\n\n### Question\n\nIs there a way of setting the current index? I'm creating a picker which lists files in the same directory as the current file (`%:h`) and would like to automatically \"select\" that file when the picker starts. This is so that I can quickly navigate to files that are next to each other (`foo.go` and `foo_test.go` or `foo.c` and `foo.h`) while still being able to choose another file in the directory.",
    "bodyHTML": "<h3 dir=\"auto\">Contributing guidelines</h3>\n<ul class=\"contains-task-list\">\n<li class=\"task-list-item\"><input type=\"checkbox\" id=\"\" disabled=\"\" class=\"task-list-item-checkbox\" aria-label=\"Completed task\" checked=\"\"> I have read <a href=\"https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md\">CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h3 dir=\"auto\">Module(s)</h3>\n<p dir=\"auto\">mini.pick</p>\n<h3 dir=\"auto\">Question</h3>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Is there a way of setting the current index? I'm creating a picker which lists files in the same directory as the current file (<code class=\"notranslate\">%:h</code>) and would like to automatically \"select\" that file when the picker starts. This is so that I can quickly navigate to files that are next to each other (<code class=\"notranslate\">foo.go</code> and <code class=\"notranslate\">foo_test.go</code> or <code class=\"notranslate\">foo.c</code> and <code class=\"notranslate\">foo.h</code>) while still being able to choose another file in the directory.</p>",
    "bodyText": "Contributing guidelines\n\n I have read CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md\n\nModule(s)\nmini.pick\nQuestion\nIs there a way of setting the current index? I'm creating a picker which lists files in the same directory as the current file (%:h) and would like to automatically \"select\" that file when the picker starts. This is so that I can quickly navigate to files that are next to each other (foo.go and foo_test.go or foo.c and foo.h) while still being able to choose another file in the directory.",
    "category": {
        "createdAt": "2022-01-02T14:24:09Z",
        "description": "Ask the community for help",
        "emoji": ":pray:",
        "emojiHTML": "<div>🙏</div>",
        "id": "DIC_kwDOFoksYM4CAgqN",
        "isAnswerable": true,
